      {
        "position": 3,
        "title": "Great for untangling and then de-fleaing all in one handy comb",
        "text": "This comb is such a gift if your dog has curly-ish hair like our poodle’s. This is my second one because I have one upstairs and one downstairs now. I use the large tooth side to untangle the curls before I run the tighter flea comb side through his hair. It works great and saves so much time having to switch back and forth between the brush and the flea comb. Occasionally if I don’t use the larger tooth side first, the fine teeth do snag and bend sometimes. I use a little twisty tie to loop around the bent part and pull it back into place and it’s good as new. Unlike other combs made of darker plastic, this one’s white background allows you to actually see the fleas. The handle is also sturdy and easy to grip. Great product.",
        "rating": 5,
        "date": "October 28, 2024",
        "author": "M Kuethen",
        "author_link": "https://www.amazon.com/gp/profile/amzn1.account.AE2D43K6L2ZG25ER46ARPUFD3BZA?ref=cm_cr_dp_d_bdcrb_top",
        "author_image": "https://m.media-amazon.com/images/S/amazon-avatars-global/default._SX48_.png",
        "helpful_votes": "One person found this helpful",
        "verified_purchase": true
      },
      {
        "position": 4,
        "title": "Excellent choice",
        "text": "Went through several broken plastic combs to finally buying a metal flea comb. Glad I did. I have two Australian shepherds. One side combs the long, fluffy hair around the neck. The other fine side gets the under coat so it doesn’t matte. The fine side is great for fleas, dander and flea dirt. Unlike the plastic combs, you can quickly smash the fleas with your thumb against the metal tines.",
        "rating": 5,
